- consider what happens when one packs actions into a tar file and deletes action files when bucse is running
- strange error when using ssh destination with no proper entry in authorized_keys present
- historic snapshot option
- docs:
 - README:
  - setup guide
  - example usage
  - comparison to other software
  - contribution guide
 - whitepaper
- correctly implement RENAME_EXCHANGE flag
- warning when using poor password strength (zxcvbn-c?)
- investigate a memory leak in libfuse when using fsstress
- handle addToDynArray failures
- local destination: use inotify (IN_CLOSE_WRITE, IN_MOVED_TO on actions/) to learn about new
  action files instead of listing the directory; keep the full listing every check_interval
  as a fallback (it's needed when the repository is on a network or synced filesystem, which
  inotify doesn't see, and after an IN_Q_OVERFLOW)
- local and ssh destinations: stat actions/ before listing it and skip the listing when its
  mtime hasn't changed; list again when the mtime is very recent (mtime granularity can be 1 s,
  a file added in the same second would be missed)
- retry rejected action files without relying on a full listing (each destination keeps a list
  of the rejected ones), needed once listings get rarer (inotify, mtime check)
- ssh destination: optionally run inotifywait on the server over an ssh exec channel (needs a
  Linux server with inotify-tools and shell access, not sftp-only); fall back to listing
- s3 destination. To decide first:
  - listing only new action files with ListObjectsV2 StartAfter needs action file names that
    sort by time (e.g. a timestamp prefix) -- repository format change, discuss first; needs a
    margin for clock differences between clients and an occasional full listing
  - no directory mtime in S3; S3 event notifications (SNS/SQS/EventBridge) need extra AWS
    setup and aren't available in S3-compatible stores -- probably not worth it
  - HTTP library (libcurl?) and AWS request signing (openssl) -- a new dependency
- implement undo actions (undoAction() in actions.c is a stub, so out-of-order actions are
  applied again on top of the later ones, e.g. "doAction: file already exists" in test11)
- no fsync() in the local destination: after a power loss an action file may be saved without its blocks
- aes encryption backend (AES-256-CBC) has no integrity check (MAC); consider authenticated encryption, e.g. AES-GCM

Code review findings (2026-11-13), fix one by one:

Serious:
- [x] 1. heap overflow on oversized action/storage/tar data from the server: decrypt
      functions don't check *outSize (bucse-mount.c:81, decryptBlock, tar.c:75) -- crypto-related, discuss first
- [x] 2. after a shrinking truncate the last block keeps its old length; later writes to it fail
      with EIO once the block is not cached (flush.c:116-127)
- [x] 3. flushFile resets truncSize before I/O; a failed flush makes the next one truncate to 0 (flush.c:88-91)
- [x] 4. ssh: sftp_write errors never detected (size_t res < 0, dest_ssh.c:197); sftp_close/fclose
      results unchecked -- action may point to a missing block
- [x] 5. ssh tick: read error -1 passed on as a huge size_t (dest_ssh.c:802)
- [x] 6. create.c:60 calls bucse_open_guarded() while holding bucseMutex -- deadlock
- [x] 7. use-after-free of json tokener in error messages (bucse-mount.c:350/354, 434/438)
- [x] 8. pthread_join on a never-created tickThread (bucse-mount.c:610)
- [x] 9. int overflow for files > 2 GB (flush.c:220/286/309, truncate.c:76)
- [x] 10. a remote EditFile drops local unflushed writes (actions.c:146-147); RemoveFile/RemoveDirectory
      free entries with pending writes/children

Medium:
- [x] 11. read-only mode: O_RDONLY|O_TRUNC sets PendingTrunc (open.c:58); flushFile doesn't check confIsReadOnly()
- [x] 12. actions sorted by time only (unstable qsort, microsecond timestamps); renameFile gives two actions
      the same time; dedup assumes identical actions are adjacent
- [x] 13. AddDirectory doesn't check for an existing entry; out-of-order replay duplicates directories
      (and addFile/addDirectory now check for a directory/file of the same name)
- [x] 14. renaming a dir onto an existing empty dir fails with EEXIST (rename.c:162)
- [x] 15. serializeAction checks jsonNewAction instead of jsonNewContent (actions.c:553)
- [ ] 16. bucse-init ignores putRepositoryJsonFile result, exits 0 without a repository argument,
      misreports missing option arguments
- [x] 17. action files that fail to open/read are still marked handled (both destinations)
- [ ] 18. terminal echo not restored when the passphrase fgets fails (bucse-mount.c:543)
- [ ] 19. "FUSE library version" prints the bucse version (bucse-mount.c:237)
- [ ] 20. design.md: a truncated CBC file doesn't always fail decryption (JSON parsing rejects it)
- [ ] 21. PBKDF2-SHA1 with 1 iteration -- crypto-related, discuss first
- [x] 22. tests/allTests.sh doesn't run test9-test12; test9-test11 use unencrypted action files
      from tests/data, so they only work with -e none (allTests.sh uses aes)
- [ ] 24. stressTest1 (5.2 GB cp) kills bucse-mount on a machine with 1.9 GB RAM, before any block is
      stored; suspected OOM: bucse_write buffers up to 10240 pending writes (~1.3 GB with 128 KB
      writes) before flushing. Look at it together with item 9. Note: the stress tests also need
      ~16 GB of free disk.
- [x] 25. tests are slow because the local destination looks for new action files only every
      10 s (TICK_PERIOD_SECONDS, dest_local.c): every waitForActionsHandled()/waitForFiles() costs
      up to 10 s (test11 takes ~70 s even without valgrind). Add an option (e.g. for bucse-mount)
      to shorten the interval and use it in the tests
- [x] 26. a shrinking truncate followed by a write past the new end, flushed together, keeps the
      old bytes between them instead of zeros (flushFile ignores truncSize when it reads the old
      blocks): 1000 x "A", ftruncate(10), pwrite("X", 20) reads back as 20 x "A" + "X"
- [ ] 27. a new local action can get an earlier time than actions already applied (gettimeofday()
      steps back, or this client's clock is behind another's), so every client applies it before
      them: edit then (clock steps back) delete leaves the file with its old content after a
      remount and on other clients; deleting a file just added by a client whose clock is ahead
      leaves it in place. addAction() then also leaves actions unsorted by time. Fix: a Lamport
      clock, the time of a new action = max(getCurrentTime(), time of the last action in
      actions + 1), instead of getCurrentTime() at the 6 places that create actions (no format
      change). Concurrent actions that haven't seen each other keep an arbitrary but consistent
      order. A client whose clock is far ahead pushes everyone's later times (and mtimes) after it

Typos and misleading messages:
- [ ] 23. log message typos/copy-paste errors (dest_ssh.c, unlink.c, rmdir.c, actions.c, bucse-mount.c,
      create.c, filesystem.c, rename.c, flush.c, dest.h, encr.c, tar.c); add a printf format
      attribute to logPrintf and fix the %d/size_t mismatches it reports
